\title{Proof of the Master Theorem}
\date{2026-10-23T19:52:00Z}
\tag{algorithms}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\p{We now have the tools we need to prove the [Master Theorem](00Q1).}

\proof{
Let's think about the recursion tree corresponding to a divide-and-conquer algorithm that at each stage makes #{a} recursive calls on inputs of size #{n/b}, and does #{\Theta(n^d)} extra work besides the recursive calls.

\ul{
  \li{The tree has height #{\log_b n}.}
  \li{A node at level #{k} has size #{n/b^k} and does #{(n/b^k)^d} work.}
  \li{There are #{a^k} nodes at level #{k}, so the total work at level #{k} is ##{a^k \cdot \left(\frac{n}{b^k}\right)^d = n^d \left(\frac{a}{b^d}\right)^k.}}
}

\p{Hence the overall total work is ##{\sum_{k=0}^{\log_b n}
  n^d \left(\frac{a}{b^d}\right)^k = n^d \sum_{k=0}^{\log_b n}
\left(\frac{a}{b^d}\right)^k.} This is #{n^d} times a [geometric series](00Q0) with ratio #{a/b^d}.}

\ul{
  \li{If #{a < b^d} then the ratio is #{< 1} and the sum is a constant; hence the total work is #{\Theta(n^d)}.}
  \li{If #{a = b^d} then the ratio is #{1} and the sum is #{\Theta(\log_b n)}; hence the total work is #{\Theta(n^d \log n)}.}
  \li{If #{a > b^d} then the ratio is #{> 1} and the sum is proportional to its final term, #{(a/b^d)^{\log_b n}}. In that case the total work is ##{\Theta\left( n^d \cdot \left(\frac{a}{b^d}\right)^{\log_b n} \right) =
    \Theta \left( n^d \cdot \frac{a^{\log_b n}}{(b^{\log_b n})^d}\right) = \Theta(a^{\log_b
      n}) = \Theta(n^{\log_b a}).}}
}

}
